 "signs": [
  {
   "x": 33,
   "y": 8,
   "text": "THE MOOT. All ten Stones are sworn to sit here, one midsummer in every generation. The Four Winds keep the gates and the Champion keeps the centre. Please wipe your boots on the chalk. The chalk does not mind."
  },
  {
   "x": 34,
   "y": 14,
   "text": "THE HEEL STONE. On midsummer morning the sun rises just beside this stone, line-straight with the centre of the circle. The builders of 4,500 years ago did the sums with sticks, string and an exceptional patience."
  },
  {
   "x": 39,
   "y": 47,
   "text": "VISITOR CENTRE. Pop in for the tea, the exhibition, and the full-size model of a Neolithic house. The shuttle bus is the only way to arrive that does not involve three hours of walking and a small crisis."
  },
  {
   "x": 31,
   "y": 42,
   "text": "PLEASE DO NOT TOUCH THE STONES. They are 4,500 years old, and they have been touched by far too many cows already. If a stone touches you, that is a spiritual matter."
  },
  {
   "x": 56,
   "y": 27,
   "text": "LONDON. About 90 miles by road. The Ministry of Plot Convenience has compressed this to a short path, and the cartographer wishes it noted she was not consulted. Keep walking."
  },
  {
   "x": 33,
   "y": 33,
   "plaque": "stonehenge_stone_joints",
   "title": "Carpentry in Stone",
   "text": "The lintels are fixed with joints borrowed from woodwork. Knobs on top of the uprights fit hollows cut beneath the lintels, like mortise and tenon, and the lintels of the outer ring lock end to end with tongue and groove. Neolithic builders thought like carpenters."
  },
  {
   "x": 47,
   "y": 49,
   "plaque": "stonehenge_cecil_chubb",
   "title": "Cecil Chubb",
   "text": "In 1915 Stonehenge was sold at auction, and a local man, Cecil Chubb, bought it for 6,600 pounds, reportedly as a present for his wife. Three years later he gave it to the nation. Whether she minded is not recorded, though it was a tricky one to wrap."
  }
 ],
